\pard\qc\b\fs32 Philosophy of Science\par
\pard\qc\b0\fs24 Pre-Socratic Philosophers Study Guide\par
\pard\par

\pard\b\fs28 1. Thales of Miletus (c. 624-546 BCE)\par
\pard\b0\fs24
Thales is often considered the first philosopher in Western tradition. He proposed that water (hydor) was the fundamental substance (arche) underlying all of reality.\par
\par
Key ideas:\par
\pard\li720
- Everything is made of water\par
- The earth floats on water\par
- All things are full of gods (animism)\par
\pard\par

\pard\b\fs28 2. Anaximander (c. 610-546 BCE)\par
\pard\b0\fs24
Student of Thales, Anaximander proposed the "apeiron" (the boundless/infinite) as the origin of all things rather than any single element.\par
\par
Key ideas:\par
\pard\li720
- The apeiron as fundamental principle\par
- First to draw a map of the known world\par
- Proposed early theory of evolution\par
\pard\par

\pard\b\fs28 3. Heraclitus (c. 535-475 BCE)\par
\pard\b0\fs24
Known for his doctrine that change is central to the universe. Famous for saying "You cannot step into the same river twice."\par
\par
Key ideas:\par
\pard\li720
- Fire as the fundamental element\par
- Unity of opposites\par
- Logos (cosmic reason/order)\par
- "Panta rhei" - everything flows\par
\pard\par

\pard\b\fs28 4. Parmenides (c. 515-450 BCE)\par
\pard\b0\fs24
In direct contrast to Heraclitus, Parmenides argued that change is an illusion and that reality is one, eternal, and unchanging.\par
\par
Key ideas:\par
\pard\li720
- Being is; non-being is not\par
- Change is impossible (logical argument)\par
- The Way of Truth vs The Way of Opinion\par
\pard\par
